WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.000
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to "Deconstructing Chatbots."

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.410
I am Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.350
And in this episode, we will
dissect the architecture

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.275
of chatbots to better
understand the building blocks.

00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:12.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.190
In the last episode, we
introduced Dialogflow,

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.990
which is an end-to-end
tool powered

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.690
by natural language
understanding to facilitate

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.390
rich and natural conversations.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.560
Today let's look at a
high-level architecture.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.690
Dialogflow sits in the
middle of the stack.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.270
A user can interface with it
via all the common channels,

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:43.540
including text, websites, apps,
messengers, and smart voice

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.090
devices like Google Home.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.360
Dialogflow handles the job of
translating natural language

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.050
into machine-readable data
using machine learning

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.870
model trained by your examples.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.930
Once it identifies what
the user's talking about,

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.650
it can hand this
data to your back-end

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.620
where you can use it
to make stuff happen.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.660
At the back-end you
can fulfill the request

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.560
by integrating with your other
services, databases, or even

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.190
third party tools like your CRM.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:18.010
Now let's dig one layer deeper
into the Dialogflow piece

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.570
itself.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.310
We first create an
agent within Dialogflow.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.910
Agent is basically your
entire chatbot application--

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.330
the experience of collecting
what the user's saying,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.380
mapping it to an intent,
taking an action on it,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.170
and then providing the
user with the response.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.160
And in your agent, this all
starts with the trigger event

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.310
called utterance.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.220
This is how our users
invoke the chatbot.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.400
So if I say, hey
Google, play some music,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.720
the whole sentence is utterance.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.130
While the phrase, hey
Google, is the trigger.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.080
Let's take another example.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.900
Hey Google, talk
to Smart Scheduler.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.420
The phrase, talk
to Smart Scheduler,

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.630
is the invocation
phrase for our chatbot.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.960
And Smart Scheduler here
is the invocation name.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.200
Once the bot is activated
and has collected the user

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.220
utterance, we need to understand
what the user's intent is.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.530
Why do they want
to talk to our bot?

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.530
So when you say, I want
to set an appointment,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.100
set appointment
here is the intent.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.310
Or if you ask what are
your hours of operation,

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.250
then hours of operation
is the intent.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.770
To control all this,
you provide Dialogflow

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.260
with different examples
of users' intent

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.190
like set an appointment, hours
of operation, and others.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.430
Dialogflow then trains
the machine learning model

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:47.480
with many more similar
phrases and finally maps

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.330
the user's phrase
to the right intent.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.460
This process is called
intent matching.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.370
Now that we know
our user's intent

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.220
we need to know what to do
to give them a response.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.250
For that you configure
actions and parameters

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.420
to define the variables you
want to collect and store.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.280
Let's look at an example.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.100
Set an appointment
for 5:00 AM tomorrow.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.270
When a users says that,
5:00 AM and tomorrow

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.200
are two critical pieces of
information in that statement

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.690
that we would actually need
to book an appointment.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:24.780
Those variables are
defined as entities.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.530
Dialogflow offers different
types of entities.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.845
And we will cover those in
detail in an upcoming episode.

00:03:30.845 --> 00:03:33.410
Once we have the
variables, we may

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.740
use them to provide a
static response to the user.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.040
Or in most cases, we may
want to send the variable

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.770
to our back-end, take
some action on it,

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.890
and then provide a user
with a dynamic response.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.910
We will look at
that in a second.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:48.410
OK.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:53.300
To summarize, an intent includes
training phrases, actions

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.360
and parameters, and response.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.760
Depending on what
services your bot offers,

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.350
you might typically have from
few to thousands of intents.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.210
They could also be in
different languages.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.430
While you're looking
at the architecture,

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.800
it's worth mentioning context.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.880
Context is the method for your
chatbot to store and access

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:18.410
variables so it can exchange
information from one intent

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.269
to another in a conversation.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.800
We will have more information on
context in an upcoming episode.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.110
Fulfillment is the last
piece in the puzzle.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.540
It's the code that
you write to interface

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.960
with the back-end services to
respond to a dynamic request.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.880
We will play with
this a lot more soon.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.510
But for now, note
that Dialogflow

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.020
has inbuilt integration
with Google Cloud Functions

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.150
to interface with your back-end.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.380
And you can also provide
an other HTTPS endpoint,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.720
and Dialogflow will
just connect to it.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.220
All right.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.740
So today we looked at a
simple high-level architecture

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.380
of setting up an agent
within Dialogflow

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.220
and learned about intents,
entities, context,

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.590
and fulfillment at a high level.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:07.190
Next we are going to go hands-on
and build a simple appointment

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.020
scheduler chatbot.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.480
If that sounds exciting
to you, join me

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.390
in the next episode of
"Deconstructing Chatbots."

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:18.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

